WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.977
The first lab step creates a small dataframe.

00:00:02.977 --> 00:00:07.002
Read the variable names before focusing on the code syntax.

00:00:07.002 --> 00:00:09.477
Patient ID is an identifier.

00:00:09.477 --> 00:00:13.212
Triage level and membership tier are ordered categories.

00:00:13.212 --> 00:00:15.502
Temperature is an interval measurement.

00:00:15.502 --> 00:00:18.272
ER visits is a ratio count.

00:00:18.272 --> 00:00:22.277
Run this in Jupyter, Colab, or VS Code.

00:00:22.277 --> 00:00:29.662
We are deliberately using a tiny dataset so you can inspect the values manually while learning the concepts.

00:00:29.662 --> 00:00:34.937
When reading the example, do not read punctuation one character at a time.

00:00:34.937 --> 00:00:41.297
Start with the purpose of the query, then identify the input rows, the operation being applied,

00:00:41.297 --> 00:00:43.737
and the shape of the expected output.

00:00:43.737 --> 00:00:50.807
In a real setting such as a university analyzing student records, surveys, and learning-platform activity,

00:00:50.807 --> 00:00:57.012
this habit makes code reviews much easier because you can explain what the query is supposed to guarantee

00:00:57.012 --> 00:00:59.207
before discussing syntax.

00:00:59.207 --> 00:01:05.520
After running it, inspect a few records manually and check that the result matches that explanation.
